       \leq e^{\sigma(t-s)+a}
 \qquad(0\leq s\leq t\leq m).$$

outputs.exact_statement:
For the process above, put $$v_l=\mathbb EA_{l-1}^2-\mathbb EA_l^2,\qquad l\geq 1.$$ Then $v_l\geq 0$, $\sum_{l\geq 1}v_l\leq\mathbb EY_0^2$, and $$\label{eq:dyadic-convolution}
 \|Z_h\|_{L^2}
 \leq \frac12\sum_{a=0}^{h-1}2^{-a/2}\sqrt{v_{h-a}}
 \qquad(h\geq 1).$$ Consequently $$\label{eq:dyadic-square-sum}
 \sum_{h\geq 1}\|Z_h\|_{L^2}^2
 \leq C\,\mathbb EY_0^2$$ for an absolute constant $C$. The same estimates hold for a block beginning at any deterministic integer time.

For each $\eta>0$ there is a positive sequence $(b_k)_{k\geq1}$ with $$\label{eq:b-summability}
 b_k\geq 2^{-k/10},
 \qquad
 \sum_{k\geq1}b_k^2<\infty,$$ and compact sets $\widetilde{\mathcal G}_k\subset\mathcal F M$ and $\mathcal G_k=\pi(\widetilde{\mathcal G}_k)\subset M$ such that $$\widetilde\nu(\widetilde{\mathcal G}_k)\geq1-\eta/100,
 \qquad \nu(\mathcal G_k)\geq1-\eta/100.$$ For $n=2^k$, $b=b_k$, and $e\in\widetilde{\mathcal G}_k$, define $$A_t=R(\widetilde S^t e)\quad(-n\leq t<n),
 \qquad
 \lambda_j=\frac1{2n}\sum_{t=-n}^{n-1}\ln(A_t)_{jj}.$$ Then for every $j$ and every integer $-n\leq u\leq n$, $$\label{eq:good-chord}
 \left|\sum_{t=-n}^{u-1}\ln(A_t)_{jj}
          -(u+n)\lambda_j\right|\leq bn.$$ Consequently, on every subinterval $-n\leq u\leq v\leq n$, $$\label{eq:good-subinterval}
 \left|\sum_{t=u}^{v-1}\ln(A_t)_{jj}
          -(v-u)\lambda_j\right|\leq2bn.$$

Fix $d\geq1$ and $K\geq1$. Let $0\leq p\leq d$, let $m\geq1$, and let $A_0,\ldots,A_{m-1}$ and $\widehat A_0,\ldots,\widehat A_{m-1}$ be real $d$-by-$d$ matrices such that $$\|A_t\|\leq K,\qquad
 \|\widehat A_t-A_t\|\leq\epsilon\leq1.$$ Use the same product notation as above for nonnegative indices. Suppose $a\geq0$, $\sigma\in\mathbb R$, and $$\|\mathord{\bigwedge}^p A(t,s)\|
       \leq e^{\sigma(t-s)+a}
 \qquad(0\leq s\leq t\leq m).$$ There is $C=C(d,K)$ such that $$\label{eq:exterior-perturbation}
 \left\|\mathord{\bigwedge}^p\widehat A(m,0)
             -\mathord{\bigwedge}^p A(m,0)\right\|
 \leq e^{\sigma m+a}
       \left[(1+C\epsilon e^{a+|\sigma|})^m-1\right].$$ For $p=0$ the left side is zero.*

*In particular, use the scales <a href="#eq:smooth-scales" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:smooth-scales">[eq:smooth-scales]</a>, take $m\leq2n$, $a\leq C_1bn$, $|\sigma|\leq C_2$, and $\epsilon\leq C_3e^{-3Bn/2}$, with fixed constants. Then for every fixed $C_0>0$ the left side of <a href="#eq:exterior-perturbation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:exterior-perturbation">[eq:exterior-perturbation]</a> is $$\label{eq:relative-exterior-small}
 o\!\left(e^{\sigma m-C_0bn}\right),$$ uniformly for all matrices satisfying these hypotheses.

Let $M$ be a compact smooth manifold of positive finite dimension, let $\nu$ be a smooth positive probability volume in the density sense of [sec:introduction](#sec:introduction), and let $S:M\to M$ be a smooth diffeomorphism preserving $\nu$. Fix a Riemannian distance $d_M$ and $\eta>0$. There exist positive numbers $B_k$ with $\sum_k B_k^{16}<\infty$ and a constant $C$ such that the following holds for all sufficiently large $k$, with $n=2^k$.*



*There is a finite measurable partition $\mathcal D_k$ of $M$ with $\#\mathcal D_k\le \exp(CB_kn)$. Define the probability measure $$\label{eq:pair-law}
 \rho_k=\sum_{\substack{Q\in\mathcal D_k\\\nu(Q)>0}}
       \frac{(\nu|_Q)\otimes(\nu|_Q)}{\nu(Q)}.$$ Thus one first samples a cell with its $\nu$-weight and then samples two independent points in that cell. There are a measurable set $G_k\subset M\times M$, with $\rho_k(G_k)\ge1-\eta$, and a measurable map $z_k:G_k\to M$ such that $$\label{eq:switch-law}
 (z_k)_*(\rho_k|_{G_k})\le 2\nu$$ and $$\label{eq:switch-shadow}
 \sup_{(x,y)\in G_k}\left[
 \max_{0\le t\le n}d_M(S^t z_k(x,y),S^t x)
 +\max_{-n\le t\le0}d_M(S^t z_k(x,y),S^t y)
 \right]\longrightarrow0.$$ The measure on the left of <a href="#eq:switch-law" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:switch-law">[eq:switch-law]</a> is not normalized after restriction to $G_k$. The same conclusions hold if $M$ has a smooth boundary.

For every sufficiently large $k$ and every $(x,y)\in G_k$, the first two equations of <a href="#eq:four-switch-equations" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:four-switch-equations">[eq:four-switch-equations]</a> have a solution $z$ with $$\label{eq:sharp-shadow}
 \max_{0\le t\le n}d_M(S^tz,S^tx)
 +\max_{-n\le t\le0}d_M(S^tz,S^ty)
 \le e^{Cgn}r.$$ In fixed regular coordinates along these halves the solution is unique among displacement arrays of sup norm at most $\delta$. The complementary solution $w$ has the analogous properties with $x,y$ exchanged. In the product coordinates of the common cell, if $x=(x_s,x_f)$ and $y=(y_s,y_f)$, then $$\label{eq:ideal-switch}
 z=(y_s,x_f)+o(r),\qquad w=(x_s,y_f)+o(r),$$ uniformly over all retained pairs. Both outputs remain in the original product cell, and their relevant endpoints remain in the respective four original endpoint bins.

Let $R_i=\ell_i/m_i$. Every $m_i$ is a power of two, with $$\log m_i=\sum_{j<i}K_j.$$ The rates satisfy $$\label{eq:word-rate-decay}
  R_{i+1}\le\frac{\varepsilon_i}{100}R_i,
  \qquad R_i\longrightarrow0.$$ They also have the lower bound $$\label{eq:word-rate-lower}
 R_i\ge R_1\,200^{-(i-1)}
          2^{-(i-1)(i+4)/2}
     \ge R_1\,2^{-(i-1)(i+20)/2},$$ and $$\label{eq:word-rate-divergence}
  \sum_{i=1}^{\infty}K_iR_i^{16}=\infty.$$

The law of <a href="#eq:aligned-list" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:aligned-list">[eq:aligned-list]</a> under $\mu$ is the law under $\mu_i$ of the first $D-1$ level-$i$ words beginning at zero. Under $\mu_i$, the index $J=a_{i+2}/m_i$ is uniform on $\{0,\ldots,N_iN_{i+1}-1\}$ and is independent of $V_{i+2}$. More precisely, for each such $j$ and $v\in\mathcal W_{i+2}$, $$\label{eq:parent-slot-law}
 \mu_i(J=j,V_{i+2}=v)
 =\frac{\mu_{i+2}(V_{i+2}=v)}{N_iN_{i+1}}.$$

For all sufficiently large $i$, for every power of two $2\le D\le D_i^*$, and every integer $t$, $$\label{eq:window-entropy}
 H_\mu(\mathcal L_{i,D}(\,\cdot\,,t))
 \ge .996(D-1)\ell_i,\qquad \ell_i=\log L_i.$$ The large-$i$ threshold is independent of $D,t$ and of the invariant probability on $X$.

Let $(B_k)_{k\ge1}$ be any positive sequence with $\sum_k B_k^{16}<\infty$. There exist levels $i\to\infty$ and powers of two $D$ satisfying $$2^{\lceil K_i/16\rceil}\le D\le2^{\lfloor K_i/8\rfloor}$$ such that, for $n=m_iD$, $$\label{eq:relative-cost}
 \frac{B_{\log n}}{R_i}\longrightarrow0,
 \qquad R_i=\frac{\ell_i}{m_i}.$$ In particular any partitions with $\log\#\mathcal D_k\le C B_k2^k$ obey $$\label{eq:cheap-partition}
 \log\#\mathcal D_{\log n}=o((D-1)\ell_i)$$ on this sequence, for every fixed $C$.

For all sufficiently large dyadic times in the smooth switching estimate, the retained pairs for which $$\begin{aligned}
 \#\{0\le t<n:f(S^tz)\ne f(S^tx)\}
       &<d_*n/100,\label{eq:future-symbol-match}\\
 \#\{-n\le t<0:f(S^tz)\ne f(S^ty)\}
       &<d_*n/100\label{eq:past-symbol-match}
\end{aligned}$$ have original $\rho$-mass at least $.96$.
